BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech4dev-ai-cohort-program-3-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Tech4Dev: AI Cohort Program 3.0 (NGOs building AI
  solutions)
DESCRIPTION:The third round of Project Tech4Dev's AI Cohort\, a hands-on im
 plementation programme for NGOs designing\, building and deploying respons
 ible AI solutions inside their own programmes. Cohort 2.0 worked with seve
 n NGOs across education\, civic engagement\, youth development and nonprof
 it technology\, who built and piloted AI teaching assistants\, volunteer c
 oaches\, mentoring tools and decision-support systems\; the organisers' st
 ated lesson was that adoption turns on understanding user needs\, testing 
 rapidly\, measuring impact and building responsibly rather than on the tec
 hnology itself. Cohort 3.0 is aimed at organisations ready to move past ex
 ploration into solutions that can be deployed in real-world settings. STRU
 CTURE (three sprints over roughly six months): Discovery Sprint (months 1 
 to 2) covering problem discovery\, use-case prioritisation\, data-readines
 s assessment\, user research and solution design\; Build Sprint (months 2 
 to 5) covering prototype development\, implementation sprints\, user testi
 ng\, mentorship and responsible AI evaluations\; and Activation Sprint (mo
 nth 6) covering pilot deployment\, impact assessment\, operationalisation 
 planning and a final showcase with peers and ecosystem partners. SELECTION
  CRITERIA: NGOs must show some in-house technical or data capability\, ide
 ally a dedicated person who can anchor the work (not necessarily an AI exp
 ert\, but able to manage workflows\, communicate operational requirements 
 and work with mentors and experts)\; must commit one person's full bandwid
 th for the whole programme\, including aligning leadership\, adapting oper
 ations and making room to test\, monitor and refine\; and preference goes 
 to problems involving multi-modal input\, especially Indic languages\, and
  to use cases built on LLMs. Applications are open to nonprofits across se
 ctors\, with priority to climate-focused organisations whose AI use cases 
 could improve programme delivery\, decision-making or community outcomes. 
 Apply via the Google Form linked from the announcement before 10 August 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Project Tech4Dev\nLocation: Remote cohort programme. 
 No geographic restriction is stated in the call\, but the framing is India
 -centred: preference goes to problems involving multi-modal input and Indi
 c languages.\nAward: FLAG\, UNVERIFIED: no grant amount\, stipend\, fee or
  compute credit is stated. What the call describes is hands-on implementat
 ion support\, mentorship and expert guidance across three sprints\; partic
 ipating NGOs must invest one person's full bandwidth for the duration.\nDu
 ration: Six months\, tentatively mid-September 2026 to January or February
  2027 (subject to change)\n\nMore: https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-p
 rogram-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-scale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-program-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-sc
 ale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:Remote cohort programme. No geographic restriction is stated in th
 e call\, but the framing is India-centred: preference goes to problems inv
 olving multi-modal input and Indic languages.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collider-program-manager-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260823
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260824
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collider: Program Manager (AI safety fieldbuilding\, New 
 York)
DESCRIPTION:Collider\, the New York coworking and convening space for AI sa
 fety and other high-impact work\, is hiring a Program Manager to design\, 
 run and scale the programmes that grow the AI safety talent pipeline and i
 nfrastructure in New York. The role owns programmes end to end\, from conc
 ept to launch to iteration\, working with researchers\, practitioners\, ed
 ucators\, funders and partner organisations. WHAT YOU DO: design and opera
 te fieldbuilding programmes (fellowships\, training programmes\, workshops
 \, conferences\, cohort courses and new formats)\; own budget\, timeline\,
  vendor relationships\, participant experience and post-programme evaluati
 on\; recruit and coordinate mentors\, instructors\, advisors and participa
 nts\, and build the pipelines and selection processes that hold quality as
  things scale\; define and track outcomes with metrics beyond participant 
 satisfaction\, and iterate on the evidence\; maintain relationships across
  research labs\, university groups\, training programmes (MATS\, ARENA\, B
 lueDot\, SPAR\, AI Safety Camp are named)\, grantmakers and adjacent commu
 nities\; represent Collider externally\; write programme reports\, strateg
 y memos\, post-mortems and public materials\; and work with leadership on 
 which programmes to launch\, scale or sunset. REQUIRED: 3+ years of progra
 mme or project management\, ideally running cohort\, fellowship or trainin
 g programmes\; substantive understanding of the AI safety field\, its orga
 nisations\, its main research agendas (alignment\, interpretability\, eval
 uations\, governance) and its open problems\; strong operational instincts
 \; an entrepreneurial\, initiative-taking approach to judgement calls abou
 t people\, partnerships and priorities\; and clear written communication. 
 NICE TO HAVE: prior experience at an AI lab\, AI safety organisation\, uni
 versity group\, training programme or grantmaker\; a background in educati
 on\, talent development or community building\; enough ML or alignment lit
 eracy to follow a paper's argument\; policy literacy on the AI governance 
 landscape\; or a background in finance\, law or another professional field
  Collider wants to build pipelines into. PROCESS: apply via the form in th
 e job document by 23 August 2026 (applications are reviewed on a rolling b
 asis and an offer may be made before the deadline\, so applying early is a
 dvised)\, then a 15 to 25 minute screening call with hiring partner Impact
  Ops\, an interview with the prospective manager\, a paid one-day in-perso
 n work trial at the Collider coworking space\, and 2 to 3 references. Repo
 rts to the Director.\n\nOrganisation: Collider\nLocation: Hybrid in New Yo
 rk City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates must already have the right
  to work in the US.\nAward: USD 150\,000 to 180\,000 base salary\, plus he
 alth insurance\, 401(k) and 20 days paid time off. The one-day in-person w
 ork trial is paid.\nDuration: Full-time\, permanent\; start date flexible\
 n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX
 4WYDSy7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX4WYDSy
 7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hybrid in New York City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates mus
 t already have the right to work in the US.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gmap-digital-art-culture-lab-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
SUMMARY:Deadline: G.MAP Digital Art Culture Lab 2026: Project Lab (Gwangju\
 , South Korea)
DESCRIPTION:The Gwangju Media Art Platform (G.MAP)\, an institution of Gwan
 gju\, UNESCO Creative City of Media Arts\, invites artists and teams worki
 ng in digital art to the 2026 Digital Art Culture Lab: Project Lab\, a fiv
 e-month creation support program (August to December 2026) covering the en
 tire creative process from idea development through production\, showcase 
 and archiving. WHO CAN APPLY: artists and groups in digital art wishing to
  create interdisciplinary projects using digital technology (all members a
 ged 19+\; international applicants welcome). Focus areas: digital art\, me
 dia art\, generative art\, AI\, interactive art\, XR\, AR\, VR\, interdisc
 iplinary\, art and technology. SELECTION: 14 teams total across two tracks
 : Track 1 Digital Transformation (7 artists/teams) and Track 2 Interdiscip
 linary Creation (7 teams). INTERNATIONAL ARTISTS CAN APPLY FOR TRACK 2 ONL
 Y. SUPPORT: creation and production grant up to KRW 30\,000\,000 per team 
 for Track 2\, two online mentoring sessions\, and a showcase in G.MAP exhi
 bition spaces and on large-scale public media platforms including the G.MA
 P LED Wall and city media facades. KEY DATES: application period 21 July t
 o 9 August 2026\, 6 PM KST\; results announced 14 August 2026\; program pe
 riod 20 August to 31 December 2026. Overseas participants (Track 2) must a
 ttend two in-person meetings in Jeonnam-Gwangju. HOW TO APPLY: submit the 
 application form and portfolio (PDF) by email to uartlabseoul@gmail.com. M
 ore info at urbanartlab.org/opencall. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Gwan
 gju Media Art Platform (G.MAP)\nLocation: Jeonnam-Gwangju\, South Korea. I
 nternational applicants welcome (Track 2 only).\nAward: Track 2 (Interdisc
 iplinary Creation): creation and production grant up to KRW 30\,000\,000 p
 er team\, plus accommodation\, equipment\, studio space\, production and t
 ravel support\, exhibition on G.MAP spaces and large-scale public media pl
 atforms (LED Wall\, city media facades)\, and publication. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: Five-month creation support program\, August to December 2026\n
 \nMore: https://urbanartlab.org/opencall/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://urbanartlab.org/opencall/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Jeonnam-Gwangju\, South Korea. International applicants welcome (T
 rack 2 only).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:antikythera-agentworld-journal-cfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Antikythera Journal: Agentworld Special Issue\, Call for 
 Papers
DESCRIPTION:Antikythera\, a research programme on planetary computation and
  the evolution of intelligence\, invites contributions to Agentworld\, a s
 pecial issue of its peer-reviewed MIT Press journal launching Fall 2026. T
 HEME: Agentworld is a research initiative on the medium-term future of hyb
 rid human-AI societies\, framed not around tools for agentic task completi
 on but around what the brief calls 'the human perplexity of deep agentic d
 iffusion'. It treats human societies as the evolutionary niches into which
  new artificial species must adapt\, where the constraints of agentic inte
 gration are qualitative and open-ended (psychological\, philosophical\, li
 nguistic\, symbolic\, existential) rather than utility functions to be sol
 ved. The framing describes a 'parasociety' of densely reticulated social g
 raphs in which agent-to-agent communication constitutes most socially sign
 ificant interaction\, humans and agents anthropomorphise each other by inh
 eritance rather than necessity\, and the two overlapping societies act as 
 mutual evolutionary niches. The disciplinary posture proposed is 'a preemp
 tive anthropology of open-world centaur societies': building the vocabular
 y\, scenarios and conceptual armature for an emergent sociality while it i
 s still forming\, rather than describing it after the fact. WHAT THEY WANT
 : theoretical essays and technical papers that engage the Agentworld theme
 s directly and explain how the contribution adds to the special issue's co
 re questions. Philosophical\, theoretical\, technical\, design-led and pro
 duct-oriented research are all in scope. The call explicitly favours work 
 that exceeds conventional academic journal formats\, including contributio
 ns combining philosophical argument\, technical analysis\, design research
 \, diagrams\, software\, infrastructure proposals\, visual media or web-na
 tive formats. Contributors may optionally propose still images\, diagrams\
 , video\, web-based or interactive components\, and should say whether the
 y have the design\, development or production capacity to realise them\; t
 hese elements are not required. SUBMIT: an abstract or proposal of up to 5
 00 words\; author name(s)\, affiliation(s)\, contact details\, biography(i
 es) and CV(s)\; a designated lead author or contact person for collaborati
 ve submissions\; and a portfolio with at least one relevant work sample su
 ch as a preprint\, published article or paper. Full paper drafts are encou
 raged where available but not required at the initial stage. Submissions m
 ay be individual or collaborative. TIMING: the review window opened 25 Jun
 e 2026. NOTE: no closing date is published on the call page\; the deadline
  shown here is a placeholder set roughly a month out\, so check the site o
 r submit early.\n\nOrganisation: Antikythera (Antikythera: Journal for the
  Philosophy of Planetary Computation\, published with MIT Press)\nLocation
 : Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-forward)
 \nAward: Unpaid. No honorarium or fee is offered for accepted contribution
 s\; the return is peer-reviewed publication in the MIT Press-published Ant
 ikythera Journal. No submission fee.\nDuration: Single publication\; first
  selection of articles published Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://agentworld.ant
 ikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#ca
 ll-for-papers
URL:https://agentworld.antikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral#call-for-papers
LOCATION:Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-fo
 rward)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: EUR 2m funding and learning pro
 gramme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
 ning-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://europeanaifund.org/newspublications/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-
 building-narrative-power-funding-and-learning-programme/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Organisations registered in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia
 \, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, G
 ermany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtens
 tein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Po
 rtugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. Th
 e UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries are not eligib
 le for this programme.
