UID:govai-research-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI Research Fellows and Research Scholars (AI Governan
 ce) 2026
DESCRIPTION:GovAI is hiring for two research roles with a single shared app
 lication (apply once to be considered for both). RESEARCH FELLOWS play a c
 entral role in GovAI's research: experienced researchers who conduct indep
 endent\, high-quality work to inform high-stakes decisions in government a
 nd industry\, whether through policy memos\, blog posts\, academic publica
 tions or strategic advising\, alongside mentoring early-career researchers
  (such as Summer and Winter Fellows) and helping shape GovAI's research ag
 enda. Two-year contract with the possibility of renewal\; Fellows work und
 er a Senior Research Fellow or leadership-team member but have substantial
  autonomy over projects\, agendas and publication formats. RESEARCH SCHOLA
 RS join for a one-year visiting position designed to support the career de
 velopment of AI governance researchers and practitioners: conducting polic
 y\, social science or technical research\, advising policymakers\, or star
 ting new applied projects. TOPICS include risk management\, threat modelin
 g\, economics of AI\, geopolitics of AI\, public policy and technical gove
 rnance\; candidates working on adjacent topics are also welcome. LOCATION:
  London or Washington DC preferred\, but open to candidates based elsewher
 e (for example the Bay Area). ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of academi
 c and professional backgrounds (computer science\, political science\, pub
 lic policy\, economics\, philosophy\, law\, plus government/industry/civil
 -society experience)\; no strict educational requirement\, but the stronge
 st Fellow candidates typically have multiple years of relevant research or
  policy experience and a track record of high-quality\, impactful work. PR
 OCESS: written application\; a paid remote work test (some workstreams add
  a research agenda and a second paid test)\; then a final stage of two int
 erviews and reference checks. Deep expertise in technical governance\, fro
 ntier safety design\, cybersecurity or US policy is especially welcome. De
 adline 23:59 Anywhere on Earth\, Sunday 16 August 2026. Contact recruitmen
 t@governance.ai with location or access questions.\n\nOrganisation: Centre
  for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK or Washington\, D
 C preferred\; open to candidates based elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship avai
 lable\; US sponsorship only in exceptional cases.\nAward: Salary by experi
 ence\, role and location. Research Fellow: London GBP 84\,000-103\,500\; W
 ashington DC USD 134\,500-170\,000. Across both roles the combined range s
 tarts at GBP 75\,000 (London) / USD 100\,000 (DC)\, with the lower bounds 
 applying to the Research Scholar position. Plus pension or 401(k) contribu
 tion and benefits (health\, dental and vision insurance\; annual wellbeing
  and professional-development budgets\; coworking allowance for remote sta
 ff\; flexible hours\; extended parental leave\; 33 days paid vacation incl
 uding public holidays). No application fee.\nDuration: Research Fellow: 2-
 year full-time contract\, renewable. Research Scholar: 1-year visiting pos
 ition.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK or Washington\, DC preferred\; open to candidates base
 d elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship available\; US sponsorship only in except
 ional cases.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:konior-research-assistant-ai-ukraine-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260723
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:Deadline: Paid Research Assistant: AI and the War in Ukraine
DESCRIPTION:Bogna Konior\, Assistant Professor of Media Theory at NYU Shang
 hai\, is hiring a paid research assistant for a project on Ukraine and art
 ificial intelligence. The work supports a forthcoming book with MIT Press\
 , plus an independent scenario she is writing on the future of AI with the
  war as a lens. WHAT THE WORK LOOKS LIKE: going month-by-month through new
 s archives (New York Times\, Reuters\, Financial Times\, and specialist ou
 tlets)\, annotating and cataloguing coverage of AI\, drones\, and autonomo
 us systems in the war\, plus other developments she will direct you to\; b
 uilding and maintaining a structured Notion database covering chronology\,
  companies\, industries\, and key actors\; and organizing and editing her 
 existing notes. WHO SHE IS LOOKING FOR: interest in and general knowledge 
 of AI\; prior research and annotation experience\, careful and detail-orie
 nted\, no sloppy citations\; comfortable with Notion and with AI tools\; U
 krainian language skill a strong plus (not required)\; familiarity with AI
 \, war\, tech\, and geopolitics news sources is great. Remote-friendly\, s
 tart ASAP. She will also consider people with no prior experience\, as wel
 l as current and previous students. TERMS: USD 18 per hour\, 10-15 hours p
 er week\, flexible schedule. TO APPLY: email bogna@nyu.edu with a short in
 troduction and background (link to website\, socials\, CV\, LinkedIn\, or 
 portfolio if you have one)\; where you are based and your weekly availabil
 ity\; and one recent AI-related story\, company\, or development you find 
 interesting and why (a vibe check). Bonus: a sample of past research or wr
 iting if you have it.\n\nOrganisation: Bogna Konior (NYU Shanghai)\nLocati
 on: Remote-friendly.\nAward: USD 18 per hour\; 10-15 hours per week\, flex
 ible schedule.\nDuration: Ongoing part-time research assistantship\; start
  as soon as possible.\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com/p/Dak-bwACIMt/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/Dak-bwACIMt/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote-friendly.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-grain-cookbook-critical-ai-literacy-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN: Grain\, Open Call for Critical AI Literacy Rec
 ipes
DESCRIPTION:Grain is a cookbook of critical and creative AI literacy exerci
 ses for educators in film and the wider audiovisual and creative field\, c
 o-produced by AIxDESIGN and the Netherlands Film Academy and led by Nadia 
 Piet. It publishes in March/April 2027\, digital and print\, under CC BY-S
 A 4.0. The current open call is to propose a recipe by the end of August 2
 026 and join as a launch contributor. WHAT THEY WANT: tried-and-tested gro
 up exercises you have actually run with people in teaching or workshops\, 
 specifically in the context of film\, audiovisual work\, time-based media 
 and storytelling. Hands-on or analog\, 15 minutes or a full day\, beginner
  or advanced\; the one hard requirement is that another educator can read 
 your recipe and run it themselves. What that might look like: hands-on exp
 eriments with specific AI tools (image generators\, chatbots\, code assist
 ants\, video models)\; analog activities that reveal computational logic t
 hrough embodied practice\; critical readings\, watchings or listenings wit
 h structured discussion\; provocations\, games or role-plays that get at h
 ow AI sees\, thinks or makes\; and exercises that help people work out how
  they want to relate to AI in their own practice. WHAT MAKES A GOOD RECIPE
 : it helps a room of people think together and trusts the room to be smart
  given the right container\; it emerges from real practice rather than an 
 untested idea\; it is specific about what participants do\, step by step\,
  so a stranger could pick it up and adapt it to their group\, time\, tools
  or context\; it has at least one designed social moment where the thinkin
 g happens between people rather than privately\; it is about something lar
 ger than technique (how a machine sees an image\, where your values bump u
 p against what the tools want from you\, what a film's rhythm looks like l
 aid out in space instead of time)\; it takes a position without telling pe
 ople what to think\; and it points beyond itself with references\, reading
  and reference projects. Published example recipes include Image Forgery: 
 Archival Mimesis with AI (Artefacto)\, Machine Ways of Seeing (Nadia Piet)
 \, AI Film Now: A Close Watching Exercise (Nadia Piet) and Outside the Tim
 eline: Spatial Grid + Visual Clustering (Artefacto)\, alongside a root rec
 ipe template. WHAT LAUNCH CONTRIBUTORS GET: your recipe in the first editi
 on\, print and online\, credited to you\; a print copy when it lands and a
 n invite to the launch event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\; support turning
  your exercise into a recipe\, via Recipe Press (their LLM helper)\, the c
 ook-along\, or directly with the team\; and a need-based fee of EUR 150 pe
 r recipe for freelance and independent practitioners\, which you request i
 n the proposal form. After this round contributions stay open but web-only
 : no fee\, no dedicated support\, no print. COOK-ALONG: one live 30-minute
  online session on Wednesday 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST\, where you brin
 g a rough exercise and turn it into a recipe on the spot with Recipe Press
  (sign up at luma.com/84w148in). HOW IT WORKS: (1) propose your recipe thr
 ough the proposal form (who you are\, what the exercise is\, where you hav
 e run it\, and whether you are requesting the fee)\; a short pitch is enou
 gh\, and if it is already written you can just link your draft\; (2) they 
 reply on a rolling basis\, with one reply covering both whether it is a ma
 tch for the first edition and the fee if you requested it\, so the earlier
  you send the earlier you hear back\; (3) once it is a yes\, write it up u
 sing the structure on the Contribute a Recipe page\, with Recipe Press or 
 at the cook-along. TIMELINE: proposals open until the end of August 2026\,
  reviewed as they come in\; accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 
 2026\; Grain publishes March/April 2027. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\
 n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-produced with the Netherlands Film Academy
 \nLocation: Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anyw
 here\nAward: Need-based fee of EUR 150 per recipe for freelance and indepe
 ndent practitioners: say so in the proposal form if making the time would 
 be hard without it\, and it is settled in the same reply. Launch contribut
 ors also get their recipe in the first edition of Grain in print and onlin
 e\, credited to them\, a print copy when it lands\, an invite to the launc
 h event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\, and support turning the exercise int
 o a recipe. Published under CC BY-SA 4.0\; you keep authorship. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: One-off contribution: proposals by end of August 2026
 \, accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 2026\; Grain publishes Ma
 rch/April 2027\n\nMore: https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cook
 book-for-Critical-AI-Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cookbook-for-Critical-AI-
 Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-kb-national-library-ai-exhibition-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN x KB: Open Call for Sessions\, (Art)works and P
 erformances
DESCRIPTION:AIxDESIGN and the KB (Koninklijke Bibliotheek\, the National Li
 brary of the Netherlands) are building an exhibition and public programme 
 about AI\, made from the national collection\, at the KB in The Hague. Nad
 ia Piet is Creative Lead with the AIxDESIGN community\, co-presented with 
 the KB. The premise: long before neural nets\, libraries were the machines
  we built to organise human knowledge\, and the show uses the KB's collect
 ion to look at what AI is\, where it comes from\, and how it connects to t
 he older story of how we store\, sort\, and search what we know. Part of t
 he show lives in the KB Atelier\, with talks\, workshops\, and screenings 
 alongside\, intended as AI literacy you can wander into. WHAT THEY WANT: w
 ork that engages AI in some way (critical\, creative\, hands-on\, or playf
 ul)\; bonus if it also speaks to archives\, libraries\, or how we store an
 d sort knowledge\, though you do not need to touch the KB's collection to 
 take part. You may pitch to more than one track: (A) SOMETHING FOR THE OPE
 NING NIGHT\, a performance\, talk\, screening\, tour\, ritual\, booth\, ga
 me\, or other experience for the November 2026 opening\, fee EUR 1\,500 to
  2\,000\; (B) A SESSION IN THE EVENTS PROGRAMME\, a workshop or other sess
 ion once during the three-month run\, around 2.5 to 4 hours\, fee EUR 2\,0
 00 to 2\,500\; (C) AN (ART)WORK FOR THE EXHIBITION\, existing work in any 
 format (installation\, sculpture\, zine stand\, a corner of your own) stur
 dy enough for a public area with daily traffic\, honorarium EUR 200 to 400
  including licensing and production\; (D) SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY\, pitch 
 it anyway. WHO: artists\, designers\, researchers\, technologists\, perfor
 mers\, musicians\, archivists\, or educators working around AI\, data\, li
 braries\, or cultural heritage\; no finished idea or polished CV needed\, 
 just a clear\, specific pitch. You can be based anywhere and they especial
 ly welcome people outside the Netherlands\; the show is in-person in The H
 ague and the fee stays the same wherever you are coming from. HOW TO APPLY
 : send a short pitch through the form (which track\, your idea in a paragr
 aph or two\, roughly what it would need\, and a link to your work)\; takes
  about five minutes\, no full application required. Deadline: end of Augus
 t 2026. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-p
 resented with the Koninklijke Bibliotheek (KB)\, National Library of the N
 etherlands\nLocation: KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition a
 nd programme)\; pitches welcome from people based anywhere\nAward: Fees by
  track: opening-night experience EUR 1\,500 to 2\,000\; a session in the e
 vents programme EUR 2\,000 to 2\,500\; an existing (art)work for the exhib
 ition EUR 200 to 400 honorarium (including licensing and production). The 
 fee stays the same wherever you are based.\nDuration: Exhibition runs Nove
 mber 2026 to February 2027\; opening event November 2026\n\nMore: https://
 nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-works-More-38f
 37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-wor
 ks-More-38f37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition and programme)\
 ; pitches welcome from people based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-us-ai-policy-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI U.S. AI Policy Program
DESCRIPTION:The GovAI U.S. AI Policy Program is a bipartisan\, part-time\, 
 12-week program designed to accelerate or launch careers in U.S. AI policy
 . Open to U.S. policy professionals across government and think tanks\, it
  builds a technically-informed understanding of the AI policy landscape th
 rough guided discussion sessions\, with an emphasis on general-purpose AI.
  Topics include how general-purpose AI systems are developed\; inputs to A
 I development such as energy\, chips\, and data centers\; AI's effects on 
 productivity\, scientific discovery\, and economic growth\; national secur
 ity and great power competition\; corporate self-governance\; AI regulatio
 n in other jurisdictions\; and federal and state AI regulatory proposals i
 n the U.S. Participants also have weekly opportunities to question world-l
 eading experts\; prior guest speakers have included White House OSTP staff
 ers\, former frontier-lab employees\, and people who helped draft U.S. sta
 te- and federal-level AI policy proposals. The program runs early Septembe
 r to early December 2026 (break for Thanksgiving week) with a roughly 5-ho
 ur weekly commitment: two hours of synchronous evening sessions (EST)\, he
 ld in-person at GovAI's D.C. office near DuPont Circle or remotely with ca
 meras on\, plus three hours of independent reading and podcasts from a fre
 e syllabus. To list completion on a resume\, participants must attend 9 of
  12 sessions\, write a short policy memo or op-ed\, and pass an interview-
 based exam. Eligible participants include executive and legislative branch
  staff (fellows\, interns\, detailees\, military personnel\, national labo
 ratory personnel) and think-tank policy researchers and affiliates\, acros
 s a range of seniority levels\, with preference for the D.C. metropolitan 
 area\; participants need not currently cover AI but should aspire to a car
 eer in U.S. tech or national security policy. Selection has two stages: an
  online application with six biographical questions\, a resume\, two verba
 l reasoning questions\, and an approximately 7-minute video\; followed by 
 a 30-minute interview in July\, with final decisions in early August.\n\nO
 rganisation: Centre for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: Washington
 \, D.C.\, USA (hybrid\; remote participation available)\nAward: Free to pa
 rticipate\; in-person attendees provided dinner (value kept at or below US
 D 20 per session)\nDuration: 12-week part-time program\, early September t
 o early December 2026 (approximately 5 hours per week)\n\nMore: https://ww
 w.governance.ai/post/govai-u-s-ai-policy-program?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/post/govai-u-s-ai-policy-program?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C.\, USA (hybrid\; remote participation available)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ford-foundation-technology-fellow-ai-philanthropy-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
SUMMARY:Deadline: Technology Fellow: AI and Philanthropy
DESCRIPTION:The Ford Foundation seeks a two-year Technology Fellow for AI a
 nd Philanthropy\, an internally-facing role focused on cross-programmatic 
 learning and advisory support. Reporting to the Director\, Technology and 
 Society\, the AI Tech Fellow will support Foundation staff in building and
  adapting internal frameworks for evaluating AI use in ways that prioritiz
 e mission and strategy and address ethical\, sociotechnical\, and operatio
 nal considerations. Responsibilities include designing and curating hands-
 on trainings\, workshops\, and learning sessions to help staff build pract
 ical familiarity with AI tools and critically assess their value and effic
 acy\; creating accessible documentation and guides on mission-aligned AI e
 xperimentation\; curating sociotechnical conversations on how philanthropy
  and the nonprofit sector are experimenting with AI in ways that respect p
 rivacy\, safety\, and equity\; monitoring emerging global AI developments 
 and providing timely briefings to program teams\; conducting landscape res
 earch on AI initiatives in the philanthropy and social justice sectors\; a
 nd building relationships with AI researchers\, practitioners\, grantees a
 nd partners working on mission-enabling AI. The Technology Fellows Program
  is administered by Ford's Technology and Society Program and is designed 
 to increase sociotechnical capacity within the Foundation. Requirements in
 clude a minimum of 7 years working within public-interest\, government\, c
 ivil society\, or commercial technology sectors\; a baccalaureate degree o
 r equivalent experience\; demonstrated sociotechnical expertise in artific
 ial intelligence and related technologies including machine learning syste
 ms and generative AI and their societal implications\; and experience desi
 gning\, implementing or supporting AI applications and tools in a complex 
 global setting. Hybrid role based in New York (three days per week in offi
 ce). Not eligible for visa sponsorship\; candidates must be authorized to 
 work in the United States. Submit both a resume and a cover letter to be c
 onsidered.\n\nOrganisation: Ford Foundation\nLocation: New York\, New York
 \, USA\nAward: USD 166\,000 to 170\,000 annual salary\, plus comprehensive
  benefits and retirement contributions of up to 13%\nDuration: Two-year te
 rm-limited contract\n\nMore: https://fordfoundation.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/
 en-US/FordFoundationCareerPage/details/Technology-Fellow--AI-and-Philanthr
 opy_R1294?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fordfoundation.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/en-US/FordFoundationCareer
 Page/details/Technology-Fellow--AI-and-Philanthropy_R1294?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York\, New York\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:genesis-mission-ai-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Genesis Mission AI Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Horizon Institute for Public Service\, Renaissance Philanth
 ropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science are partnering to launch a fellowship 
 that embeds technical AI talent across the US Department of Energy nationa
 l labs to advance the goals of the Genesis Mission\, DOE's flagship scienc
 e and technology initiative launched in late 2025. The Genesis Mission ide
 ntifies 26 national science and technology challenges critical to US leade
 rship and envisions a national platform connecting supercomputers\, experi
 mental facilities\, AI systems\, and unique datasets\, with DOE's 17 natio
 nal labs playing a key role. Genesis Mission AI Fellows are forward deploy
 ed to DOE national labs to bring frontier AI expertise to high-priority sc
 ientific challenges. As core members of the technical staff on a Genesis M
 ission project\, fellows work alongside national lab scientists and partne
 rs and apply AI to national priorities\, from building cutting-edge models
  of plasma dynamics in pursuit of controlled fusion to advancing state-of-
 the-art models for foundational materials discovery. Initial national lab 
 partners include Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National Laborator
 y\, and the Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory\; additional partners may 
 be announced later. COMPENSATION/DURATION: 9-12 months starting fall 2026\
 , with a prorated total annual compensation of USD 200\,000\; fellows are 
 expected to travel to their national lab site at least once a quarter but 
 may work on-site if they prefer. ELIGIBILITY: competitive applicants shoul
 d have at least 2 years of industry experience building AI models or agent
 s (those with industry experience building AI applications or data infrast
 ructure will also be considered)\; scientific domain expertise is not requ
 ired\, but any placement will involve some training and study in a specifi
 c scientific discipline or energy topic\, supported by expert colleagues a
 t the host institution. All fellows must be eligible to work in the United
  States for the duration of the placement\; US employment visas cannot be 
 sponsored\, but US permanent residents (green card holders) are eligible. 
 APPLICATION: initial form requires a CV or resume (max 2 pages) and short-
 answer questions on motivations and experiences (max 200 words)\; successf
 ul applicants complete interviews through partner organizations and nation
 al labs\, and selected fellows are matched to partner labs by scientific d
 omain and availability. The review uses evidence-based best practices incl
 uding blind grading of application materials. TIMELINE: application deadli
 ne 31 July 2026\; final fellow selection August-September 2026\; expected 
 start fall 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Horizon Institute for Public Service (wi
 th Renaissance Philanthropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science)\nLocation: Uni
 ted States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of Energy nation
 al labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National
  Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or in-person\; 
 on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to work in the US
  (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).\nAward: Prorated 
 total annual compensation of USD 200\,000.\nDuration: 9-12 months\, starti
 ng fall 2026.\n\nMore: https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-gen
 esis-mission-ai-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-genesis-mission-ai-fell
 owship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of En
 ergy national labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhav
 en National Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or i
 n-person\; on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to wor
 k in the US (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
