UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transfyr-ai-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Transfyr AI Fellowship 2026-2027 (Boston/Cambridge)
DESCRIPTION:A one-year\, full-time research fellowship for graduate student
 s\, recent PhDs and postdocs to lead a project on frontier machine learnin
 g problems grounded in how scientific work actually happens. THE PREMISE: 
 Transfyr argues science is missing an observability layer. Lab work is ful
 l of tacit decisions\, failed attempts\, operator judgment\, noisy instrum
 ents and context that never reaches the written protocol\, and the company
  is building what it calls the world's largest multimodal dataset of scien
 tific execution for models to reason over. Fellows get to work with that d
 ata. RESEARCH AGENDA\, eight named directions: multimodal reasoning (align
 ing video\, audio\, protocol text\, instrument traces\, timestamps\, gaze 
 and outcomes)\; conflicting evidence (uncertainty and calibration\, eviden
 ce attribution\, contradiction-aware reasoning)\; long-context scientific 
 memory (long-horizon state tracking\, retrieval over process histories\, f
 ailure and deviation memory)\; real-world evaluations (benchmark design\, 
 process-level metrics\, lab-grounded model assessment)\; tacit expertise (
 expert-novice comparison\, skill representation\, human-in-the-loop feedba
 ck)\; physical-world AI (embodied data pipelines\, transfer across setting
 s\, policy and assistance evaluation)\; biosecurity and bio-risk observabi
 lity (synthesis-to-execution verification\, emergent capability tracking\,
  autonomous vulnerability detection)\; and scientific execution ontology (
 wet-lab-grounded metadata design\, cross-domain causal attribution\, multi
 -scale variability mapping). WHAT FELLOWS DO: define a research question\,
  build prototypes\, analyse real multimodal scientific-execution data\, pr
 essure-test evaluation ideas\, and produce a publishable or demo-grade art
 ifact\, with direct mentorship from Transfyr's founders\, technical team a
 nd advisor network across AI\, biology\, robotics and scientific operation
 s. WHO CAN APPLY: current graduate students able to take leave (and to sel
 f-certify that they can commit full-time)\, recent PhDs and postdocs in ma
 chine learning\, computer science\, computational biology\, robotics\, hum
 an-computer interaction\, statistics or adjacent fields. Strong ML fundame
 ntals and evidence of research taste are the bar\; the call asks for comfo
 rt with ambiguity and curiosity about how science happens in physical envi
 ronments. International applicants are welcome and visa support is availab
 le for selected fellows. IP TERMS\, worth reading before applying: Transfy
 r owns the fellowship work product and supports publication subject to con
 fidentiality and IP review. This is a company fellowship\, not an academic
  one. APPLICATION: a one-page application sent to ai_fellowship@transfyr.a
 i saying what you want to work on and what resources you need\, with links
  that make your work easy to evaluate\; references are requested of finali
 sts. DEADLINE: applications are reviewed on a ROLLING basis\; apply by 15 
 August 2026 for full consideration\, decisions roll through late August 20
 26\, fellowship begins around September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Transfyr\nL
 ocation: Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remo
 te considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable
 .\nAward: $125\,000 annual compensation plus benefits\, plus access to com
 pute\, GPUs\, data and project support. Applicants are asked to specify th
 e compute\, GPU\, data or support their project would need. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Full-time for 12 months\n\nMore: https://fellowship.trans
 fyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fellowship.transfyr.ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boston/Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US\, full-time in person. Remot
 e considered by exception when the project and candidate make it workable.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-special-projects-roles-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: Special Projects Roles in AI Governance (2 to 5 hir
 es)
DESCRIPTION:IAPS is hiring 2 to 5 special projects roles to support or own 
 new workstreams in AI governance\, helping the world navigate towards a po
 sitive future with transformative AI. LEVELS AND SALARY: Special Projects 
 Manager\, USD 122\,000 to 165\,000\; Senior Associate\, USD 97\,000 to 126
 \,500\; Associate\, USD 86\,000 to 101\,000. LOCATION: Washington\, D.C. o
 r the Bay Area\, with London open to exceptional candidates\; IAPS can spo
 nsor US visas. PROJECT AREAS THEY ARE EXCITED ABOUT: (1) Bay Area to DC in
 terface\, closing the gap between where frontier AI is built and where it 
 is governed by surfacing what is happening in AI labs and technical circle
 s for DC policy audiences\, and vice versa\; (2) AI security fieldbuilding
 \, bridging a national security community short on people who deeply under
 stand frontier AI risk and an AI community short on people who understand 
 national security\, through workshops\, convenings and partnerships in DC 
 that put technical experts from both worlds in the same room\; (3) DC-base
 d special projects\, leveraging IAPS's research output and policy networks
  to build talent pipelines and the wider field of AI governance\, drawing 
 on a new 45-seat office in Dupont Circle and a 30-person fellowship cohort
  arriving in DC every six months\; and (4) programs targeting senior polic
 y audiences\, bringing policy entrepreneurs and technical professionals in
 to existing and developing research areas such as institutional resilience
  for advanced AI or AI and defence\, with participants working semi-indepe
 ndently to scope and explore new policy domains. DEADLINE: applications ar
 e considered on a rolling basis\, with applications sent by 8 September 20
 26 prioritised. HOW TO APPLY: apply at iaps.ai/special-projects. Questions
  to careers@iaps.ai.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for AI Policy and Strategy
  (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington\, D.C. or the Bay Area\, USA\; London consid
 ered for exceptional candidates. US visa sponsorship available.\nAward: Sp
 ecial Projects Manager USD 122\,000 to 165\,000\; Senior Associate USD 97\
 ,000 to 126\,500\; Associate USD 86\,000 to 101\,000. Between 2 and 5 role
 s are being filled.\nDuration: Full-time\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/spec
 ial-projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/special-projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C. or the Bay Area\, USA\; London considered for ex
 ceptional candidates. US visa sponsorship available.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:normsy-social-media-measurement-methods-incubator-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Normsy: Social Media Measurement Methods Incubator RFP
DESCRIPTION:Normsy\, a project of Civic Health Project\, runs a small metho
 ds incubator for new strategies to measure or estimate the impact of proso
 cial social-media interventions. Normsy identifies toxic posts (typically 
 content attacking or violating civic norms) and offers real users a set of
  AI-generated prosocial comments to choose from and post\; the open questi
 on is how to estimate the effect of those comments on content producers\, 
 other commenters and bystanders who consume content without interacting me
 asurably. The current approach combines survey experiments (how active X u
 sers respond emotionally to threads with and without the intervention\, an
 d how it shifts agreement with and support for the original post) with pai
 d X API platform data (classifying comments before and after an interventi
 on\, plus coarse engagement metrics that cost money each time they are rec
 ollected). WHAT THEY WANT: a systematic way to leverage available platform
  data and maximise the utility of the survey experiments. Example proposal
 s include a strategy for measuring toxicity/prosociality and reach/visibil
 ity of a thread over time with a randomisation design generating a control
  group of threads\, or an approach to benchmarking the prosocial potential
  of AI-generated comments through an iterative arena with human judges or 
 a theory-backed LLM validation approach with human validation. The brief i
 s explicit that methodological innovation\, causal identification\, scalab
 le measurement frameworks and novel observational approaches matter far mo
 re than proposing new intervention types\; proposals need not study Normsy
  exclusively but must explain how the evaluation design transfers to it. E
 thics and IRB considerations are central and Normsy will collaborate with 
 selected researchers on ethics review and implementation planning. Researc
 hers retain publication independence and IP\; the partnership is expected 
 to produce data for an academic paper. SUBMIT: one zipped folder through t
 he linked Google Form containing a 3-page proposal (design\, work already 
 done\, work still needed to scale and implement)\, a budget document for t
 he USD 20\,000\, a timeline with concrete deliverables inside 6 months\, a
 nd short bios for all team members. Award notifications mid-October 2026. 
 Questions to kylan@civichealthproject.org\; informal and exploratory conve
 rsations welcome.\n\nOrganisation: Normsy (a project of Civic Health Proje
 ct)\nLocation: Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, acade
 mic-adjacent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Res
 earch context is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must inclu
 de X).\nAward: USD 1\,000 awarded to each of the top 5 proposals\, plus up
  to USD 20\,000 to fund R&D and/or testing with one selected partner indiv
 idual or team. Preference for budgets where at least 50 percent goes to re
 search costs (survey respondents\, LLM or X API credits). No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Proposals must set out concrete deliverables within 6 month
 s of starting\; the partnership is framed as the start of a potentially lo
 nger-term collaboration\n\nMore: https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, academic-adj
 acent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Research c
 ontext is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must include X).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bold-lab-bold-fellow-oxford-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260823T184115Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: BOLD Fellow\, British Open-ended Learning and Discovery L
 ab
DESCRIPTION:The British Open-ended Learning and Discovery (BOLD) Lab is a n
 ew UKRI-funded initiative establishing an AI research laboratory led from 
 the University of Oxford\, bringing more than 80 researchers from Oxford\,
  UCL and Imperial College London under one vision. It carries an initial i
 nvestment of around GBP 8 million with the potential for a further GBP 22 
 million extending the investment over six years\, making it one of the UK'
 s largest bets on fundamental AI research. The lab's remit is ambitious\, 
 high-risk work on the fundamental science of artificial intelligence\, alo
 ngside training future leaders in the field. THE ROLE: up to 12 BOLD Fello
 ws are being recruited across the three universities. Fellows are expected
  to operate with a high level of independence and responsibility\, setting
  their own research direction\, publishing at the highest level\, and ment
 oring junior researchers and doctoral students. They also play a leading r
 ole in managing the lab and take responsibility for developing its strateg
 ic and scientific direction\, with primary responsibility for a programme 
 of independent funded research projects. The posting frames the draw as ex
 ceptional independence\, direct collaboration with world-leading colleague
 s across the three institutions\, access to substantial and rapidly growin
 g compute\, and real influence over the intellectual direction and culture
  of a major national lab. WHO SHOULD APPLY: senior researchers with an est
 ablished\, original research track record in AI or a closely related field
 \, who are a recognised authority in their area. HOW TO APPLY: submit an o
 nline application with a covering letter or supporting statement\, a CV\, 
 and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed beforehand to
  the BOLD Exec Team at boldexec-team@eng.ox.ac.uk. NOTE: this is a salarie
 d fixed-term research post rather than a grant. Only applications received
  before midday on Tuesday 15 September 2026 can be considered.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: University of Oxford\, Department of Engineering Science (BOLD Lab\,
  with UCL and Imperial College London)\nLocation: Oxford\, UK\, with posts
  also based at UCL and Imperial College London. The 12 posts are employed 
 across the three universities.\nAward: Salary on Oxford Grade 10R\, GBP 65
 \,336 to GBP 84\,663 per year\, with the posting noting that further salar
 y options are being explored. Benefits include associate membership at Reu
 ben College\, 38 days annual leave\, pension scheme\, childcare services\,
  and travel expenses for stakeholder and conference visits. Fellows also g
 et access to substantial compute: 3 million GPU hours on Isambard for the 
 first 18 months\, with the lab working towards 5\,000 H100-equivalents in 
 total across industry and government support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Fixed-term\, 12 months\, funded by UKRI\n\nMore: https://eng.ox.ac.uk/j
 obs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Oxford\, UK\, with posts also based at UCL and Imperial College Lo
 ndon. The 12 posts are employed across the three universities.
END:VEVENT
